Following the definition of the stochastic and deterministic recurrences, the difference $z_{n, k} - z_{n, k+1}$ that appears in \eqref{eq:z_diff} is equal to  egin{align} z_{n, k+1} - z_{n, k} &= \esp{h(\varphi_{n-k}(	heta_{k})) - h(\varphi_{n-(k+1)}(	heta_{k+1}))}
onumber\ &= \mathbb{E} ig[h\circ\varphi_{n-(k+1)}(	heta_{k} + \alpha  ar{f}(	heta_{k}))
onumber\ &\qquad - h\circ\varphi_{n-(k+1)}(	heta_{k} + \alpha (f(	heta_{k},X_{k})+M_{k+1})) ig] \end{align} If $g:\mathbb{R}^d	o\mathbb{R}$ is a thrice differentiable function whose third derivative is bounded by $
orm{D^3g}$, by using a Taylor expansion, we have that $g(x+a) - g(x+ b) = Dg(x) (a-b) + D^2g(x) (a-b)^{\otimes2} + R$, where $R$ is a remainder term that is smaller than $ rac{c}{6}
orm{a-b}^3$. In our proof, we use this Taylor expansion with the function $g=h\circ\varphi_{n-(k+1)}$, $x=	heta_{k}$, $a=\alpha(f(	heta_{k},X_{k})+M_{k+1})$ and $b=\alpha ar{f}(	heta_{k})$. Applying this to \eqref{eq:before_taylor} shows that  egin{align} z_{n, k+1} - z_{n, k} = \alpha A_{n,k} +  rac{\alpha^2}{2} B_{n,k} + R_{n,k}, \end{align} where $A_{n,k}$ and $B_{n,k}$ are equal to  egin{align*} A_{n,k} &:= \esp{D(h\circ\varphi_{n-(k+1)})(	heta_{k})(f(	heta_{k},X_{k})+M_{k+1}- ar{f}(X_{k}))}\ B_{n,k} &:= \esp{D^2(h\circ\varphi_{n-(k+1)})(	heta_{k}) (f (	heta_{k},X_{k})+M_{k+1}- ar{f}(X_{k}))^{\otimes 2}}, \end{align*} and $R_{n,k}$ is a remainder term. By assumption ef{A:mart}, the conditional expectation and variance of the martingale term are equal to $\esp{M_{k+1}\mid \mathcal{F}_{k}} = 0$ and $\esp{M_{k+1}^{\otimes2}\mid \mathcal{F}_{k}}=Q(	heta_{k},X_{k})$. By using the total law of expectation, this shows that $A_{n,k}$ and $B_{n,k}$ can be simplified as:  egin{align*} A_{n,k} &:= \esp{D(h\circ\varphi_{n-(k+1)})(	heta_{k})(f(	heta_{k},X_{k})- ar{f}(X_{k}))}\ B_{n,k} &:= \esp{D^2(h\circ\varphi_{n-(k+1)})(	heta_{k}) \left((f (	heta_{k},X_{k})- ar{f}(X_{k}))^{\otimes 2} + Q(	heta_{k},X_{k})ight)}. \end{align*} By definition, $V^{(\alpha)}_n = \sum_{k=0}^{n-1}(A_{n,k} + \alpha B_{n,k}/2)$. Hence, combining this with \eqref{eq:z_diff} shows that  egin{align*} \esp{h(	heta_n) - h(\phi_{n\alpha}(	heta_0))} &= V^{(\alpha)}_n + \sum_{k=0}^{n-1} R_{n,k}. \end{align*} To complete the proof, it remains to be shown that there exists a constant such that $\sum_{k=0}^{n-1} R_{n,k} \le C\alpha^2$ and that $V^{(\alpha)}_n$ is bounded independently of $\alpha$ and $n$. There are three terms, corresponding to $R$, $B$, and $\sum_{k=1}^n A_{n,k}$:\ 	extbf{Case of $R$}. In Lemma~ef{lemma:exponentially_small_derivatives}, we show that the norm of the derivatives of $h\circ\varphi_{n-k}$ are smaller than $c_1 e^{- c_2\alpha (n-k)}$. As the derivatives of $h$ are bounded by $1$, this implies there exists $c$ independent of $\alpha$ and $n$ such that $
orm{D^3(h\circ\varphi_{n-k})}\le ce^{-c_2\alpha (n-k)}$. Hence, there exists $c'>0$ such that:  egin{align*} \sum_{k=0}^{n-1} 
orm{R_{n,k}}\le c' \alpha^3 \sum_{k=0}^{n-1} e^{-c_2\alpha (n-k)} \le c' \alpha^3 \sum_{k=0}^\infty e^{-c_2\alpha k} \le c'c_3 \alpha^2. \end{align*} 	extbf{Case of $B$}. The proof that $\sum_{k=1}^n \alpha B_{n,k}$ is bounded independently of $n$ and $\alpha$ is similar since the factor $\alpha$ cancels out with the factor $1/\alpha$ that comes out of $\sum_{k=0}^\infty e^{-c_2\alpha k}$. 	extbf{Case of $A$} Showing that $\sum_{k=1}^n A_{n,k}$ is bounded independently of $n$ and $\alpha$ is more subtle and uses the fact that $ ar{f}$ is the ``averaged'' version of $f$ with respect to the transition kernel $K$. The proof of this is a consequence of the first point of Lemma~ef{lem:Poisson} that is stated in Section~ef{sec:proof_lemma}.

Let us denote $u_k:=D(h \circ \varphi_{n-(k+1)})(	heta_{k})$ and $v_k:=g(	heta_k,X_k)$. The quantity \eqref{eq:kind_of_telescopic} is equal to $\esp{\sum_{k=0}^{n-1} u_k(v_{k+1}-v_{k})}$. By shifting the indices of the sum (which corresponds to a discrete-time integration by part), we get:  egin{align} \sum_{k=0}^{n-1} u_k(v_{k+1}-v_{k})&= \sum_{k=0}^{n-1} u_k v_{k+1}-\sum_{k=0}^{n-1}u_kv_{k}
onumber\ &= \sum_{k=1}^{n} u_{k-1} v_{k}-\sum_{k=0}^{n-1}u_k v_{k} \ &= u_{n-1}v_n - u_0v_0 + \sum_{k=1}^{n-1} (u_{k-1}-u_k)v_k. \end{align} As the norm of $g$ and $h$ are bounded by $1$, $
orm{u_{n-1}v_n - u_0v_0}$ and $
orm{v_k}$ are bounded independently of $\alpha$. Hence, the result follows if we can show that $\sum_{k=1}^{n-1} 
orm{u_{k-1}-u_k}$ is bounded regardless of $\alpha$. To show this, by adding and subtracting the term $D(h \circ \varphi_{n-k})(	heta_{k})$, we have:  egin{align*} u_{k-1}-u_k &= D(h \circ \varphi_{n-k})(	heta_{k-1}) - D(h \circ \varphi_{n-k})(	heta_{k}) \ &\qquad + D(h \circ \varphi_{n-k})(	heta_{k}) - D(h \circ \varphi_{n-(k+1)})(	heta_{k}) \end{align*} By Lemma~ef{lemma:exponentially_small_derivatives}, the function $D(h \circ \varphi_{n-k})$ is Lipschitz continuous with a constant $c e^{-(n-k)\alpha c_2}$. As the difference between $	heta_k$ and $	heta_{k-1}$ is of order $\alpha$, this shows that the first line is bounded by $c' \alpha e^{-(n-k)\alpha c_2}$. For the second line, the definition of the function $\varphi_{n-k}$ in \eqref{eq:discrete_rec} implies that for any $	heta$:  egin{align*} \varphi_{n-k}(	heta) - \varphi_{n-(k+1)} (	heta) &= \alpha  ar{f}(\varphi_{n-(k+1)}(	heta)). \end{align*} This shows that difference between $D(h \circ \varphi_{n-k})$ and $D(h \circ \varphi_{n-(k+1)})$ is of order $c''\alpha e^{-(n-k)\alpha c_2}$. The two facts combined imply that:  egin{align*} \sum_{k=0}^n 
orm{u_{k-1}-u_k}\le (c'+c'') \sum_{k=0}^n \alpha e^{-c_2(n-k)\alpha} \le (c'+c'')\sum_{k=0}^\infty \alpha e^{-c_2k\alpha}\le (c'+c'')c_3 \alpha. \end{align*}

To lighten the notations, we consider the case $i=1$, the proof holds for $i>1$ by replacing $D(h\circ\varphi)$ by $D^i(h\circ \varphi)$. By adding and subtracting some terms, the quantity \eqref{eq:decoupled_accuracy_lemma} is equal to the expectation of  egin{align} \sum_{k=0}^{n-1}D(h \circ \varphi_{n-k-1})(	heta_{k}) \Bigl( g(	heta_{k},X_{k})-  ar{g}(	heta_{k}) &+ G_{g}(	heta_{k}, X_{k}) - G_{g}(	heta_{k}, X_{k+1}) \ & + G_{g}(	heta_{k}, X_{k+1}) - G_{g}(	heta_{k+1}, X_{k+1}) \ & + G_{g}(	heta_{k+1}, X_{k+1}) - G_{g}(	heta_{k}, X_{k}) \Bigr). \end{align} We examine the three lines separately:  egin{itemize} \item By using the fact that $X_{k+1}$ makes a Markovian transition \eqref{eq:Markov}, and by using the definition of $G_h(x,y)$, we get:  egin{align*} \esp{G_{g}(	heta_k, X_k) - G_{g}(	heta_k, X_{k+1})\mid \mathcal{F}_k} & = -\sum_{y'} K(	heta_{k})_{X_k,y'} G_{g}(	heta_k,y') \ & = - (g(	heta_k,X_k)- ar{g}(	heta_{k})) \end{align*} from which follows that the expectation of \eqref{eq:extended_difference_lemma_decoupling_1} is equal to zero. \item To bound Equation \eqref{eq:extended_difference_lemma_decoupling_2}, we use the definition~\eqref{eq:model} of $	heta_{k+1}$ to implies that there exists a constant $C>0$ such that $\abs{	heta_{k+1} - 	heta_k} \le C\alpha $. By using that $G_g$ is Lipschitz-continuous (see \cite[Lemma~4]{allmeier2023bias}) and that the norm of the derivative of $h\circ\varphi_{n-k}$ is bounded by $c_1e^{-c_2(n-k)\alpha}$ (Lemma~ef{lemma:exponentially_small_derivatives}), this shows that there exists $c'<\infty$ such that:  egin{align*} & \esp{\sum_{k=0}^{n-1}
orm{D(h \circ \varphi_{n-k-1})(	heta_{k}) \left( G_{g}(	heta_{k}, X_{k+1}) - G_{g}(	heta_{k+1}, X_{k+1}) ight) }} \le c' \alpha \sum_{k=0}^\infty e^{-c_2k\alpha} \le c_3c'. \ \end{align*} \item The fact that the expectation of \eqref{eq:extended_difference_lemma_decoupling_3} is bounded is a direct consequence of Lemma~ef{lem:kind_of_telescopic_sum_bound} applied with $g=G_g$ and the fact that $
ormop{G_g}= O{
ormop{g}}$. \end{itemize} This conclude the proof.
